{# Who the airline is, and how to reach it, in one masthead.

   The mark used to sit on a line of its own above four tiles, two of which
   were not figures at all: the code, which is in the page's title and its
   address, and the country, which is a link rather than a number. A tile is
   for something counted, so everything that identifies the airline sits with
   the mark instead -- its name, those two, and the two ways of contacting it,
   which had been hanging below the tiles as a row belonging to nothing.

   Three rows of text give the mark something to be as tall as, which is why it
   is bigger here than the 3.5rem it was when it stood alone.

   The country is the one identity fact that goes somewhere else, so it stays a
   link. #}
<div class="masthead">
    {# Empty alt: the heading directly above says "Air Canada", so a reader who
       cannot see the mark has already been told whose page this is. Width and
       height on the tag as well as in the CSS, so nothing moves when it
       arrives. #}
    <img class="masthead__mark"
         src="{{ cdn(config('site.static.endpoint.images') ~ '/suppliers/' ~ place.code ~ '.png') }}"
         width="80" height="80" loading="lazy" alt="">

    <div class="masthead__identity">
        <p class="masthead__name">{{ place.name }}</p>
        <p class="masthead__meta">
            <span class="masthead__code">{{ place.code }}</span>
            {% if place.country is not empty %}
                <span class="masthead__sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                {# `??`, not `|default`: a row that never had the key is the
                   case to survive, and the footer's columns give the same
                   reason for the same choice. #}
                {% if place.country_url ?? null %}
                    <a class="masthead__link" href="{{ place.country_url }}">{{ place.country }}</a>
                {% else %}
                    {{ place.country }}
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}
        </p>

        {# Both are seed data and both can be missing -- 11 of the 105 have no
           website and 6 no phone number -- so each appears only where there is
           one, and the row itself only where there is either.

           The number is text and not a tel: link, which is the one place this
           row is not a link. Counted: 1 of the 105 numbers carries a country
           code. The rest are written the way each airline writes them at home
           -- "800-221-1212" is a North American toll-free that dials nothing
           from anywhere else, "213 21 98 63 6" is Algeria's code without the
           plus that would make it one, and one row ends in a stray dash. A
           tel: built from those dials a wrong number more often than the right
           one, and a wrong number is worse than a number you copy yourself. #}
        {% if place.url is not empty or place.phone is not empty %}
            <p class="masthead__contact">
                {% if place.url is not empty %}
                    {# The address itself, as stored. 88 of the 105 are https
                       and 6 are plain http; those 6 print as they are, because
                       showing https on a link that goes to http would be the
                       page claiming something it has not checked. #}
                    <a class="masthead__link" href="{{ place.url }}"
                       target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ place.url }}<i
                        class="fas fa-arrow-up-right-from-square masthead__out"
                        aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
                {% endif %}
                {% if place.url is not empty and place.phone is not empty %}
                    <span class="masthead__sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                {% endif %}
                {% if place.phone is not empty %}
                    {{ place.phone }}
                {% endif %}
            </p>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
</div>

{# Four counted things, and two of them had to earn their place.

   Destinations is top-heavy in this data -- almost every airline reaches
   almost everywhere -- but it is still the figure a traveller opens an airline
   page for. The two beside it are the ones that actually separate the 105:
   departures a day runs from 2 to 157, and the widebody share from 34% to 95%.

   A day rather than a total, because a total counts whatever the schedule
   happens to hold. See AirlineRepository::network(), which also says what was
   dropped and why -- the longest flight is about 15,000km for every airline
   here, so the tile would have said the same thing 105 times. #}
{% include 'partials/facts-grid.html.twig' with {
    'facts': [
        { 'icon': 'fa-earth-americas', 'value': place.destinations, 'label': 'Destinations' },
        {
            'icon': 'fa-plane-departure',
            'value': place.hub_count,
            'label': place.hub_count > 1 ? 'Hubs' : 'Hub'
        },
        {
            'icon': 'fa-clock',
            'value': place.per_day ?? '—',
            'label': 'Departures a day'
        },
        {
            'icon': 'fa-plane',
            'value': (place.widebody_share ?? null) is null ? '—' : place.widebody_share ~ '%',
            'label': 'Widebody flights'
        }
    ]
} only %}
